t1 ZPL Features : Common Data Structures

t2 The fantastic four

p As we said in the last chapter, in this one, we will talk about \
additionnal shortcut notations rather than real "new Z features". \
Actually, this chapter does not even cover internal Z features at \
all. The data structures we will see here are \u\b\cff0000not\c\b\u \
defined as part of the ZPL itself (as the primitive type \mboo\m we \
saw earlier for example). They must be declared by the user using for \
example a standard library before using them in the code.

p However, the ZPL includes those shortcuts as they bring simplicity \
and they shorthen the code in a good way. Moreover, those syntaxes \
are used in lots of programming languages so developpers should be \
able to get it easilly. Finally, for better flexibility, its is \
possible to tell the compiler to interpret those notations or not \
using the INTERPRET_COMMON_STRUCTURES compilation option. (By default \
set to \mON\m).

p The syntaxes we will see here concerns 4 data structures considered \
in Z as "common data structures". These are \utables\u (\mtab\m), \
\ulists\u (\mlst\m), \umaps\u (\mmap\m) and \uflies\u (\mfly\m). \
They are all collection types. As we said before, such things are not \
included in the ZPL language itself be it must be declared before \
beeing used and it is mainly the role of \ustandard libraries\u. \
They should bring you the definitions of those data structures \
alongside with lots of methods to manage them.

p \cff0000\b\uWARNING :\u\b Depending on your implementation of those \
data structures, you may have different behavior in your code. Even \
if the shortcut syntaxes are producing the same code, as a result, \
the methods called with their content are not given by the compiler. \
You may have to look into your standard library to see how things are \
made inside.\c
b

t3 Pointer collections

p The first data structure we will see is the most simple collection \
type that can be made in Z... well almost. The really most basic type \
that can be use to hold a collection of items is the primitive \
\mref\m actually. However, using it directly is not always that \
simple: storing and retrieving elements inside, managing memory \
allocation when changes occur, keeping the total collection length \
appart... Here is an example of how we can use pointers to store a \
collection of items:
" z Figure 103
//let's say we want to store 'shr' elements
ptr[shr] data   = new(8) //Let's allocate 8 bytes : Here we consider that "new()" refers to your system's memory allocation
int      length = 4      //8 bytes => 4*2 bytes (assuming here that 'shr' are allocated on 2 bytes)

//storing elements in data
data[0] =    5s //As a recall here, operator IIA used with parameters : ptr[shr],int,shr
data[1] =   -2s //This line is equivalent to a VFC_VFC ZCI like : iia(data,1,-2s)
data[2] =  120s // In order to have this working, you must have this operator defined in your LLI.
data[3] = 1289s

//retrieving an element from the collection
shr thirdItem = data[2] //Here it is the IIN operator : data[2] => iin(data,2)

//retrieving the last element
shr last = data[length-1]
"

p Don't worry about having such operators defined in your LLI because that kind of interaction with pointers are the most basic things required to work with dynamic amounts of data. \
Such operations should always be declared in a certain way in your LLI. \
In my opinion, it is very hard to work without it (not to say impossible).

p Until here, nothing seems to be really difficult because we only used our collection statically. \
The complexity goes as soon as we want to make something dynamic such as adding or removing an element. \
Here is how we can make this :
" z Figure 104
//adding an element
newData = new(length+2) //step 1 : allocate 2 bytes more
for e on length {
    newData[e] = data[e] //step 2 : refill the new data with the old content
}
newData[length] = 52s //step 3 : set the new value
length += 1           //step 4 : increase length
free(data)            //step 5 : free old content
data = newData        //step 6 : switch to new data address

//removing an element
newData = new(length-2) //step 1 : allocate 2 bytes less
for e on length {
    if e != indexOfTheElementToRemove { //step 2 : refill the new data with the old content
        newData[e] = data[e]            //         excluding the one we want to remove
    }
}
length -= 1           //step 4 : decrease length
free(data)            //step 5 : free old content
data = newData        //step 6 : switch to new data address
"

p The problem here is not the complexity of those operations, this is typically the kind of thing that should be put inside a method for that collection type. \
The problem is that we can't just add or remove a few bytes in our collection, we can only allocate a complete new collection and refill it as we want. \
This is not a problem on regular x86_64 computers with lots of memory, especially when dealing with tiny collections but when dealing with huge amount of data, \
we are not always able to allocate a new collection each time we want to add or remove a tiny part of it. \
Moreover, refilling it one by one each time make those operations really long and inefficient.


t3 Tables

p For those reasons, pointer collections are pretty useful when used as static collections. \
When creating one, we give it a length and then, we only set/get data to/from it. \
This is also what \utables\u are meant to be. \
Tables are nothing more than pointer collections with and additionnal "length" field. \
As you can see in our example, to know how many elements we have in our table, we used an external data item. \
In tables, we included it to the structure so that we can use it when imported in a function/method/operator :
" z Figure 105
typ tab[1] { int length, ptr[0] data } //declinated type of degree 1

//operator IIN for : tab[shr],int
shr [](tab[shr] t, int index) {
    if index >= t.length { ret -1s } //return -1s if getting out of bounds
    ret t.data[index]
}

//operator IIA for : tab[shr],int,shr
voi [](tab[shr] t, int index, shr value) {
    if index >= t.length { ret } //do nothing if getting out of bounds
    t.data[index] = value
}

//declare a table
myShortTable = tab { 3, ``000000000000 }

//set and get an element inside of it
myShortTable[1] = 35s
s = myShortTable[1] //35s

//set and get elements out of bounds
myShortTable[12] = 25s //doing nothing
s2 = myShortTable[12]  //-1s
"

p You see here that you are completely free of doing whatever you want with your data structures, even the most common ones. \
These are just examples of how you can manage them but the actual behavior is completely free to be anything given by you or your standard library. \
The main idea is that each common data structure has been thought in a particular way but you can actually create a structure that works completely differently if you want.

p Alright, now it is time to talk a bit about shortcut notations. \
If you remember, in previous chapters, we talked about VALUE ZCEs and the 2nd analysis with literal expressions... \
To sum up the thing, when expecting to have a VALUE ZCE, a VAP is launched which is a process divided in 2 steps : 1st and 2nd analysis. \
The first one is about ODP (operators) and the second one mainly about literal values. \
This second analysis is where common structure's shortcut notations are treated.

p Every common data structure shortcut notation is based on an includer symtax. \
One thing we did not see but that is part of the compilation process is that after transforming your Z code into a list of ZCI, \
those ZCIs are decomposed into DZCI ("Decomposed ZCI"). \
Actually, we don't analyze the integrity of ZCIs as raw text but we use their decomposed format to work with them more easilly.

p A DZCI is a serie of element that can be just text or includer elements. \
The objective is to separate includers from the rest of the raw code. \
Here is a simple example of how DZCI works : take into account includers in its 


