t3 Flies

p Files cannot be passed as function parameters. \
Flies cannot be declared without initial value. \
Flies will be replaced by a generated type at compile time.




t2 Shortcut notations

t3 Declaration

p Each of these fantastic four has a shortcut notation that can help to code faster, more clearly using symbols rather than full words. \
Those notations mainly concerns the declaration of those structures. \
Because those structures are not meant to be part of any LLI and for better flexibility, \
these shortcuts are only interpreted if the compilation option INTERPRET_COMMON_STRUCTURES in enabled.

p If you remember, in the chapter on VALUE ZCEs, we saw that in the Value Analysis Process (VAP), in the second analysis, the compiler will check for some litteral expressions. \
In these expressions, we talked about the LITERAL INCLUDERS concerning the 4 common data structures : \mtab\m, \mlst\m, \mmap\m, \mfly\m. \
Those literal expressions are what we call the "shortcut notations" for the fantastic four.

l Here are the patterns targetted to identify a VALUE ZCE as one of them :
" z Figure XX
//1 - a LITERAL tab is detected if:

    //VALUE is an empty parentheses includer
    a = ()
    //<=> (equivalent to)
    a = tab {}

    //VALUE is a parentheses includer with at least ONE coma inside
    b = (element1, element2) //element1 & element2 are interpreted as VALUE ZCEs as well
    //<=>
    //in GLOBAL scope (element1 & element2 must be constant):
    b = tab { length=2, data=``... } //concatenation of element1 & element2 constant values
    //in LOCAL scope:
    b = tab { length=2, data=new(2*#ptr) }
    b[0             ] = element1
    b[1*b->item_size] = element2


//2 - a LITERAL lst is detected if:

    //VALUE is an empty bracket includer
    a = []
    //<=>
    a = lst {}

    //VALUE is a brackets includer with at least ONE coma inside
    b = [element1, element2] //element1 & element2 are VALUE ZCEs as well
    //<=>
    //in LOCAL scope:
    b = lst { length=2 }
    b + element1
    b + element2


//3 - a LITERAL map is detected if:

    //VALUE is an empty brackets includer preceeded by a colon
    a = :[]
    //<=>
    a = map {}

    //VALUE is a brackets includer PRECEEDED BY A COLON with at least one assignment inside
    b = :[
        key1 = value1    //as soon as an assignment symbol is detected (equal sign \m'='\m not following another one),
    ]                    //both sides are considerated as other VALUE ZCEs (\i"key1"\i & \i"value1"\i here).
    //<=>
    in LOCAL scope:
    b = map { length=1l }
    b + map_couple[k,v] { key1, value1 } //here, \i"k"\i and \i"v"\i are the types of the VALUE ZCEs given
                                         //previously by \i"key1"\i and \i"value1"\i respectively


//4 - a LITERAL fly is detected if:

    //VALUE is an empty braces includer
    a = {}
    //in that case, compiler will raise an error because it makes no sens to set an empty fly.
    //  I know, that seems weird to allow a syntax that always produces an error,
    //  but this is allowed for consistency reasons :
    //  Braces includers at beginning of VALUE ZCEs are reserved for flies.

    //VALUE is a braces includer with at least one assignment inside (same thing as for maps)
    b = {
        field1 = value1 //field1 will be read as a regular field name in structure declarations
    }                   //value1 is a VALUE ZCE (associated to field1)
    //<=>
    //in GLOBAL scope:
    typ <stc> { //generated structure
        field1 = value1
    }
    b = <stc> {}
    //in LOCAL scope:
    //declare a generated structure in GLOBAL scope as before, and then, back in LOCAL scope:
    b = <stc> {} //same thing actually

    //field names are optionnal in flies, that means that you can define them with a VALUE only.
    //here is an example of 
"


